WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:07.027
The planning commissioner's job has changed more in the last ten years than any other local seat.

2
00:00:07.027 --> 00:00:14.569
On most housing projects, state law now limits the commission to objective standards, fixed deadlines, and a cap of five hearings.

3
00:00:14.569 --> 00:00:24.373
A great commissioner today knows which projects are still discretionary, writes findings that survive in court, and uses the discretion that remains as well as possible.

4
00:00:24.373 --> 00:00:29.730
Everything starts with the general plan, the city's long-range constitution for land use.

5
00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:40.203
State law requires elements on land use, circulation, housing, conservation, open space, noise, safety, and, where there are disadvantaged communities, environmental justice.

6
00:00:40.203 --> 00:00:44.837
The elements must agree with each other, and zoning must agree with the plan.

7
00:00:44.837 --> 00:00:53.865
Torrance adopted its current general plan in April 2010, built around the idea of a balanced city with a deliberate mix of homes, shops, and industry.

8
00:00:53.865 --> 00:01:01.148
A focused update now in review aligns that plan with the housing element and adds climate, heat, and evacuation policies.

9
00:01:01.148 --> 00:01:03.754
The commission's core work is entitlements.

10
00:01:03.754 --> 00:01:15.755
In Torrance, a conditional use permit requires eleven separate findings, including compatibility with the neighborhood, a suitable site, adequate access and services, and no harmful noise or hazard.

11
00:01:15.755 --> 00:01:23.695
Variances work differently here than in many cities: the commission holds the hearing and recommends, and the City Council decides.

12
00:01:23.695 --> 00:01:29.210
Whatever the entitlement, one 1974 California Supreme Court case governs how you decide it.

13
00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:38.968
Topanga Association for a Scenic Community versus County of Los Angeles requires findings that bridge the analytic gap between the raw evidence and the decision.

14
00:01:38.968 --> 00:01:41.089
In practice that means four habits.

15
00:01:41.089 --> 00:01:43.635
Track each required finding word for word.

16
00:01:43.635 --> 00:01:50.847
Point to specific evidence in the record for each one: a page of the staff report, a plan sheet, a study, or testimony.

17
00:01:50.847 --> 00:01:54.060
Explain why the contrary evidence is less persuasive.

18
00:01:54.060 --> 00:02:04.848
And when you deny a project against staff's advice, say why on the record and ask staff to bring back a written denial resolution, rather than improvising findings from the dais.

19
00:02:04.848 --> 00:02:07.413
Conditions of approval have limits too.

20
00:02:07.413 --> 00:02:17.806
Under a line of United States Supreme Court cases, a condition must have an essential connection to the project's impacts and be roughly proportional to them.

21
00:02:17.806 --> 00:02:25.371
In 2024, the Court applied that same test to impact fees adopted by legislation, not just to fees set case by case.

22
00:02:25.371 --> 00:02:26.662
Then there are appeals.

23
00:02:26.662 --> 00:02:34.074
In Torrance, a commission decision can be appealed to the council within fifteen days by the applicant or anyone adversely affected.

24
00:02:34.074 --> 00:02:38.847
Every appeal hearing counts toward the state's five-hearing cap on compliant housing.

25
00:02:38.847 --> 00:02:46.651
In court, the challenger is limited to the issues raised at the hearing or in letters delivered before it, and must sue within ninety days.

26
00:02:46.651 --> 00:02:49.290
That makes the commission's record the lawsuit.

27
00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.199
A few habits run a strong hearing.

28
00:02:51.199 --> 00:02:54.680
Read the recommended findings and conditions before the plans.

29
00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:59.902
Ask staff, on the record, which state law governs a housing item and what discretion remains.

30
00:02:59.902 --> 00:03:05.517
Ask staff questions about the code and the evidence, and ask applicants about design and operations.

31
00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:08.661
Adopt the environmental action first, then the decision.

32
00:03:08.661 --> 00:03:16.185
And when the room is hostile, explain the legal limits at the start, apply time limits equally, and explain your vote by the findings.

33
00:03:16.185 --> 00:03:20.733
For more, see the planning commissioner section of the Local Official's Handbook.
